Linear mapping. Following others [CITATION], we operationally define a neural “representation” as “linearly readable information”, specifically requiring a minimum R-squared value of 0.7 for a linear regression to be considered readable, and finding this holds true for at least 80% of neurons in visual cortex. As there is no one-to-one alignment between each MEG sensor and each activation of an LLM, we compare the representations across these two systems through a linear mapping. Specifically, we fit a ridge regression to predict LLM activations (Y∈ℝ^w× d) from brain activity (X_t∈ℝ^w× s): 
    Ŵ   = Wmin{ Y - XW _2^2 + λ W _2^2 }
 with w the number of words, d the number of LLM activations, s the number of MEG sensors and t the time point relative to word onset. For this, we use scikit-learn's , with tuning of logarithmically spaced regularization strength through a grid search approach (α = 10^-4 to 10^8, tuned for each dimension independently).

Micron-sized dust particles can be generated through the mutual collision between the large debris within the G-ring arc or by the impact of micrometeoroids onto these large bodies. After dust particles start their lives, they are subjected to various perturbation forces, including the effect of planetary oblateness, solar radiation pressure, Poynting-Roberson drag, Lorentz force, plasma drag and the gravity of moons. The equation of dust grain's motion in the equatorial inertial frame of Saturn (SIF), where the x-axis of SIF points toward the ascending node of Saturn’s orbit at J2000, the z-axis is along the spin axis of Saturn, and the y-axis is determined by the right-hand rule, is expressed as 
    r̈⃗̈   =GM_s▿{1/r[1-∑_n=1^N_SJ_2n(R/r)^2nP_2n(cosθ)]}
       +3S_fluxQ_pr/4ρ_gr_gc{[1-(r-r_sun)·S/c]S-r-r_sun/c}
       +3ϵ_0Φ/ρ_gr_g^2(V_rel×B)-3N_W^+m_W^+|V_rel|/4ρ_gr_gV_rel
       -∑[GM_moons/|r-r_moons|^3(r-r_moons)+GM_moons/|r_moons|^3r_moons],
 where r is the radius vector of the grain, M_s the mass of Saturn, R the reference radius for gravitational field of Saturn, J_2n and P_2n the Saturn’s zonal harmonics and the Legendre functions of degree 2n, and θ the colatitude of the grain in the Saturn-centered body-fixed frame. The parameters of Saturn gravity field are taken from <cit.>, where the J_2, J_4 and J_6 are considered here, i.e., N_S = 3. The variable S_flux is the solar flux at the distance of Saturn's orbit around the Sun, and Q_pr is the solar radiation pressure efficiency calculated by Mie theory <cit.>. The bulk density of the grain ρ_g = 540 kg m^-3 is assumed to be inconsistent with that of Aegaeon, differing significantly from the value reported in [CITATION]. The variable r_g is the grain size, c the speed of light, r_sun the radius vector of the Sun, and S the unit vector from the Sun to the grain.
